WHAT YOU ARE DOING, AND WHY IT MATTERS
This scene's medium has changed. Nothing downstream will rewrite the
description for you: the string you return is the ONLY instruction the
generation engine receives about motion, camera and time. A description
authored for a still and left unchanged under a moving medium produces a
moving render of a frozen idea.

RULE 1 — NO TEXT IN THE VISUAL. Absolute, and it outranks everything below.
The description must NEVER request on-screen text, numbers, equations, labels,
captions, titles, handwriting or writing of any kind. Image and video models
cannot spell or do arithmetic; they produce text-shaped marks. A real run of
this pipeline asked for "a whiteboard with a multiplication problem written on
it, such as 23 x 14" and got a whiteboard reading "2? x 23.14". Every equation,
number, label and caption is rendered by the COMPOSITION OVERLAY in a later
stage, with a real font. Describe the imagery beneath the overlay and leave
clear space where the overlay will go. Motion, camera and time are all
describable without a single digit.

RULE 2 — KEEP THE SUBJECT. You are changing the MEDIUM, not the scene. The
same step, the same working surface, the same state of the working, the same
palette, lighting and drawing style. If the current description names two
partial-product rows above a ruled line and an empty answer row, the rewrite
still has them. A rewrite the operator cannot recognise as the same scene is
a wrong answer, however good it reads.

RULE 3 — WRITE IT FOR THE TARGET MEDIUM.

  image      — COMPOSITIONAL. One frozen instant: subject, framing, the state
               of the working surface, light, palette, and the clear space the
               overlay will occupy. Remove camera moves, remove ordering words,
               remove anything that only exists over a duration. A still has
               none of those.

  video_clip — MOTION, CAMERA AND TIME, all three, in words:
                 * WHAT MOVES, as a verb: the hand sweeps down the column, the
                   pencil traces the ruled line, the counters slide into two
                   groups.
                 * WHAT THE CAMERA DOES: holds steady, drifts slowly right,
                   pushes in on the working surface, tilts down from face to
                   page. "Locked-off camera" is a legitimate answer and counts,
                   but say it.
                 * WHAT HAPPENS IN WHAT ORDER: begins with..., then...,
                   continues until..., ends holding on.... The clip has a start
                   and an end and the description must say which is which.

  animation  — THE BUILD OR THE TRANSFORM, AND THE PERSON PERFORMING IT. Say
               what accumulates, assembles, grows or changes state, and in what
               order; and name the visible human whose pose drives it. This is
               not decoration: the animation branch is pose reenactment
               (Wan2.2-Animate), it transfers a driving video's motion onto the
               SUBJECT of the scene's still, and the pipeline REFUSES a scene
               whose reference image contains no person rather than inventing a
               body. If the current description has no person in it, put one in
               - a presenter, a tutor, a demonstrator at the working surface -
               and let them perform the step.

OUTPUT
Return the rewritten visual description and NOTHING ELSE. No preamble, no
explanation, no quotation marks around it, no bullet points, no markdown, no
label. One paragraph of plain prose. If you have nothing to change, return the
current description unchanged rather than commentary about it.
